- Restarting/killing the server process (disruptive; would drop legitimate connections too and restart pattern would resume immediately as attacker reconnects — not a durable fix, and touches service state).
- Blocking `91.214.0.1` via iptables — this is an **access control / security enforcement** change and, per my policy, **requires admin approval regardless of reversibility**.
- Editing kp_webserver.py to disable `/slow` or raise workers — a config/deployment change outside my authority as the "web server host" agent; requires admin approval.

